<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sphinx</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as androsphinx</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sphinx is a mythical creature with the head of a human and the body of a lion, in some traditions with the wings of an eagle. In Greek tradition the sphinx is a merciless female being who asks those she meets a riddle and kills and eats those who fail, as in the myth and drama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a>. In Egyptian mythology the sphinx is usually male and wingless, a benevolent image of strength, often of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>; the largest is the Great Sphinx of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, whose head most Egyptologists take to bear the likeness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khafre">Khafre</a>, dating it to between 2600 and 2500 BCE. Statues of sphinxes guarded the entrances of temples and tombs, and the figure revived in European decorative art during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
